articles.content_en:
Where an individual or unit illegally manufactures, buys, sells, transports, mails, or stores firearms, ammunition, or explosives, and has any of the following circumstances, the individual or unit shall, in accordance with the provisions of Article 125, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law, be convicted and punished for the crime of illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing firearms, ammunition, or explosives:
(1) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing one or more military firearms;
(2) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing one or more non-military firearms powered by gunpowder to fire bullets, or two or more other non-military firearms powered by compressed gas or the like;
(3) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing ten or more military bullets, five hundred or more air gun lead pellets, or one hundred or more other non-military bullets;
(4) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing one or more hand grenades;
(5) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing explosive devices;
(6) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing one kilogram or more of explosives, propellants, or black powder, or three kilograms or more of pyrotechnic powder, thirty or more detonators, or thirty meters or more of safety fuse or detonating cord;
(7) a unit qualified to produce explosive articles manufactures them not in accordance with the prescribed varieties, or a unit qualified to sell or use explosive articles buys or sells, beyond the specified limits, ten kilograms or more of explosives, propellants, or black powder, or thirty kilograms or more of pyrotechnic powder, three hundred or more detonators, or three hundred meters or more of safety fuse or detonating cord;
(8) repeatedly illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing ammunition or explosives;
(9) although the above minimum quantity standards are not reached, there are other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.
Anyone who introduces the buying or selling of firearms, ammunition, or explosives shall be treated as an accomplice to the crime of buying or selling firearms, ammunition, or explosives.

Where firearms, ammunition, or explosives are illegally manufactured, bought, sold, transported, mailed, or stored, and any of the following circumstances exists, it constitutes 'serious circumstances' as provided in Article 125, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law:
(1) the quantity reaches five times or more the minimum quantity standards provided in Items (1), (2), (3), (6), and (7) of Article 1 of this Interpretation;
(2) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing three or more hand grenades;
(3) illegally manufacturing, buying, selling, transporting, mailing, or storing explosive devices, causing serious harm;
(4) reaching the minimum quantity standards provided in Article 1 of this Interpretation, and having other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.

Where an enterprise designated or determined according to law for firearm manufacturing or sales commits an act provided in Article 126 of the Criminal Law, and has any of the following circumstances, it shall be convicted and punished for the crime of manufacturing or selling firearms in violation of regulations:
(1) manufacturing five or more firearms in violation of regulations;
(2) selling two or more firearms in violation of regulations;
(3) although the above minimum quantity standards are not reached, having other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.
Under any of the following circumstances, it constitutes 'serious circumstances' as provided in Article 126 of the Criminal Law:
(1) manufacturing twenty or more firearms in violation of regulations;
(2) selling ten or more firearms in violation of regulations;
(3) reaching the minimum quantity standards provided in Paragraph 1 of this Article, and having other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.
Under any of the following circumstances, it constitutes 'especially serious circumstances' as provided in Article 126 of the Criminal Law:
(1) manufacturing fifty or more firearms in violation of regulations;
(2) selling thirty or more firearms in violation of regulations;
(3) reaching the minimum quantity standards provided in Paragraph 2 of this Article, and having other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.

Where anyone steals or forcibly seizes firearms, ammunition, or explosives, and has any of the following circumstances, the person shall, in accordance with the provisions of Article 127, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law, be convicted and punished for the crime of stealing or forcibly seizing firearms, ammunition, or explosives:
(1) stealing or forcibly seizing one or more non-military firearms powered by gunpowder to fire bullets, or two or more other non-military firearms powered by compressed gas or the like;
(2) stealing or forcibly seizing ten or more military bullets, five hundred or more air gun lead pellets, or one hundred or more other non-military bullets;
(3) stealing or forcibly seizing explosive devices;
(4) stealing or forcibly seizing one kilogram or more of explosives, propellants, or black powder, or three kilograms or more of pyrotechnic powder, thirty or more detonators, or thirty meters or more of safety fuse or detonating cord;
(5) although the above minimum quantity standards are not reached, having other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.
Under any of the following circumstances, it constitutes 'serious circumstances' as provided in Article 127, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law:
(1) the quantity of stolen or forcibly seized firearms, ammunition, or explosives reaches five times or more the minimum quantity standards provided in Paragraph 1 of this Article;
(2) stealing or forcibly seizing military firearms;
(3) stealing or forcibly seizing hand grenades;
(4) stealing or forcibly seizing explosive devices, causing serious harm;
(5) reaching the minimum quantity standards provided in Paragraph 1 of this Article, and having other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.

In any of the following circumstances, the offender shall, in accordance with the provisions of Article 128, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law, be convicted and punished for the crime of illegally possessing or privately harboring firearms or ammunition:
(1) illegally possessing or privately harboring one military firearm;
(2) illegally possessing or privately harboring one non-military firearm powered by gunpowder to fire bullets, or two or more other non-military firearms powered by compressed gas or the like;
(3) illegally possessing or privately harboring twenty or more military bullets, one thousand or more air gun lead pellets, or two hundred or more other non-military bullets;
(4) illegally possessing or privately harboring one or more hand grenades;
(5) the ammunition illegally possessed or privately harbored causes casualties or property damage.
Under any of the following circumstances, it constitutes 'serious circumstances' as provided in Article 128, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law:
(1) illegally possessing or privately harboring two or more military firearms;
(2) illegally possessing or privately harboring two or more non-military firearms powered by gunpowder to fire bullets, or five or more other non-military firearms powered by compressed gas or the like;
(3) illegally possessing or privately harboring one hundred or more military bullets, five thousand or more air gun lead pellets, or one thousand or more other non-military bullets;
(4) illegally possessing or privately harboring three or more hand grenades;
(5) reaching the minimum quantity standards provided in Paragraph 1 of this Article, and having other foul circumstances such as causing serious consequences.

The illegal carrying of firearms, ammunition, or explosives into a public place or onto public transportation, endangering public safety, and having any of the following circumstances, constitutes 'serious circumstances' as provided in Article 130 of the Criminal Law:
(1) carrying firearms or hand grenades;
(2) carrying explosive devices;
(3) carrying five hundred grams or more of explosives, propellants, or black powder, or one kilogram or more of pyrotechnic powder, twenty or more detonators, or twenty meters or more of safety fuse or detonating cord;
(4) the carried ammunition or explosives explode or burn in a public place or on public transportation, and no serious consequences have yet been caused;
(5) having other serious circumstances.
Where an actor illegally carries the explosives specified in Item (3) of Paragraph 1 of this Article into a public place or onto public transportation, and although the quantity does not reach the above standards, refuses to hand them over, the act shall be convicted and punished in accordance with Article 130 of the Criminal Law; where the quantity carried reaches the minimum quantity standards, and the actor can voluntarily and completely hand them over, it may not be treated as a crime.
